# A potentiometer dims an LED — the first program here that produces a duty
# cycle rather than an on/off level.
#
# This is the example `ledBrightness` in the board contract was specified for
# and never had: brightness is current through the LED multiplied by PWM duty,
# integrated over about 20 ms, which is what the eye does. Nothing in the
# earlier examples produces a duty at all, so half of that contract had never
# been exercised on either side.
#
#   pot   P1.2  wiper, ends to +5V and GND     (ADC channel 2)
#   lamp  P1.3  +5V -> 1k -> LED -> pin        (PCA module 0, active LOW)
#
# Those two pins are adjacent for a reason worth knowing: P1.2/P1.3/P1.4 are
# ECI/CCP0/CCP1 *and* ADC channels 2/3/4. The PWM output and the analog input
# have to be different pins, and the front end refuses it if they are not.
#
# The PWM frequency is FOSC/12/256 — about 3.6 kHz at 11.0592 MHz — which is
# far above flicker, so the eye sees a steady brightness rather than a blink.
#
# NOTE: the ADC path is written from the datasheet and has not been confirmed
# on hardware, and neither has the PCA. Both are unverified on silicon.

DEVICE STC12C5A60S2:
  CLOCK 11059200

  PIN pot  = P1.2 ANALOG                 # PDIP-40 pin 3
  PIN lamp = P1.3 PWM ACTIVE LOW         # PDIP-40 pin 4

  WHEN started:
    FOREVER:
      set lamp to pot * 100 / 1023 percent
      wait 20 ms
